    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d463">

                <pb facs="0560" n="550" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg550"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.796/10–344: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-03T17:00:00+01:00">October 3, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 3—3:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8271. ReEmbs 8267, October 3, 2 p.m. It has just been decided to postpone the
                    debate on civil aviation in the House of Lords scheduled for October 5 to
                    October 12. It is understood that the Government after further considering the
                    demand for a definition of policy which Lord Londonderry was to make on October
                    5, and after learning from him that he, as spokesman for aviation groups, would
                    not be satisfied with the partial measures mentioned in the Embassy’s 8267,
                    requested that the debate be postponed 2 weeks during which time every effort
                    would be made to come to a decision on civil aviation policy. Lord Londonderry
                    agreed to this, but on the basis of 1 week’s postponement.</p>
                <p>It is possible that within the next week the Government may discontinue its
                    present stopgap plan of announcing the appointment of an Under Secretary of
                    State for Civil Aviation and make a definite decision in favor of either a
                    separate Ministry for Civil Aviation or, in <pb facs="0561" n="551"
                        xml:id="pg551"/> any case, removing it from the Air Ministry. It is also
                    possible that during the next week the various factions will be able to agree
                    upon a definite statement concerning the repeal of the BOAC act and the
                    abandonment of the chosen instrument.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
